# Scientific Article Summaries
## AI in Pharmacovigilance and ADR Detection

### Article 1
**Topic:** Machine Learning in Pharmacovigilance

**Authors:** Kompa et al.  
**Year:** 2022

**Objective:** To review how machine learning is being applied in pharmacovigilance.

**Key findings:** Machine learning has potential for detecting safety signals and supporting drug-safety analysis.

**Limitations:** Differences in study design and validation make it difficult to compare results across studies.

**Research relevance:** Supports investigating the reliability of AI-assisted drug-safety methods.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1007/s40264-022-01176-1

---

### Article 2
**Topic:** Machine Learning for Pharmacovigilance

**Authors:** Pilipiec et al.  
**Year:** 2022

**Objective:** To review machine-learning approaches used for pharmacovigilance, including information from online text.

**Key findings:** Machine learning may help identify suspected adverse drug reactions from text-based sources.

**Limitations:** Online information may be incomplete, inaccurate, or unrepresentative.

**Research relevance:** Highlights the importance of reliable data sources.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics14020266

---

### Article 3
**Topic:** Statistical and Machine Learning Methods for ADR Analysis

**Authors:** Kim et al.  
**Year:** 2022

**Objective:** To examine statistical and machine-learning approaches for adverse drug reaction analysis.

**Key findings:** Different methods can support ADR detection, depending on the data and research question.

**Limitations:** Differences in methods and datasets make direct comparisons challenging.

**Research relevance:** Supports evaluating model performance using clearly defined criteria.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1097/MD.0000000000029387

---

### Article 4
**Topic:** Artificial Intelligence in Pharmacovigilance

**Authors:** Salas et al.  
**Year:** 2022

**Objective:** To review applications of AI in pharmacovigilance.

**Key findings:** AI may support adverse-event identification and drug-safety workflows.

**Limitations:** Data heterogeneity can affect model performance and generalizability.

**Research relevance:** Highlights the need for validation before practical implementation.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1007/s40290-022-00441-z

---

### Article 5
**Topic:** NLP-Based Adverse Drug Event Detection

**Authors:** Murphy et al.  
**Year:** 2023

**Objective:** To review supervised NLP methods for identifying adverse drug events.

**Key findings:** NLP can help identify medicine and adverse-event information in clinical narratives.

**Limitations:** Differences in annotation and evaluation methods complicate comparisons.

**Research relevance:** Supports investigating the accuracy of automated text extraction.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279842

---

### Article 6
**Topic:** Machine Learning and Exploratory Pharmacovigilance

**Authors:** Kaas-Hansen et al.  
**Year:** 2023

**Objective:** To examine machine learning for exploratory pharmacovigilance using large patient datasets.

**Key findings:** Machine learning offers opportunities for analyzing large-scale clinical data.

**Limitations:** Evidence supporting routine implementation remains limited.

**Research relevance:** Highlights the need for further validation in different settings.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1111/bcpt.13828

---

### Article 7
**Topic:** Deep Learning for Adverse Drug Event Detection

**Authors:** Li et al.  
**Year:** 2024

**Objective:** To review machine-learning and deep-learning approaches for detecting adverse drug events in clinical text.

**Key findings:** Deep-learning approaches have shown promise on benchmark text-analysis tasks.

**Limitations:** Performance on benchmark datasets may not reflect performance in every real-world setting.

**Research relevance:** Supports evaluating AI-assisted ADR detection against reference annotations.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbi.2024.104621

---

### Article 8
**Topic:** Opportunities and Challenges of AI in Pharmacovigilance

**Author:** Desai  
**Year:** 2024

**Objective:** To discuss opportunities and challenges associated with AI in pharmacovigilance.

**Key findings:** AI may assist pharmacovigilance professionals with data-intensive activities.

**Limitations:** Implementation requires appropriate oversight, reliable data, and validation.

**Research relevance:** Emphasizes human supervision in AI-assisted workflows.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.4103/picr.picr_290_23

---

### Article 9
**Topic:** NLP and Machine Learning for Adverse Drug Event Detection in Medical Records

**Authors:** Golder et al.  
**Year:** 2025

**Objective:** To review NLP and machine-learning approaches for detecting adverse drug events in electronic health and medical records.

**Key findings:** Automated text analysis may assist adverse-event detection.

**Limitations:** The available evidence and external validation remain important considerations.

**Research relevance:** Supports evaluating model performance beyond a single dataset.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1007/s40264-024-01505-6

---

### Article 10
**Topic:** AI in Pharmacovigilance Signal Management

**Authors:** Barbieri et al.  
**Publication:** Published online in 2025; journal issue details require verification.

**Objective:** To review AI tools, implementation, research, and regulatory considerations in pharmacovigilance signal management.

**Key findings:** AI may support different stages of safety-signal management.

**Limitations:** Implementation and oversight requirements need careful consideration.

**Research relevance:** Highlights the importance of transparency and responsible use.

**DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1080/14740338.2025.2545926
